#!/usr/bin/env perl
# PODNAME: sick
# ABSTRACT: Ship internal certificates and keys to the devices of a list

use strict;
use warnings;
use SICK::CLI;

our $VERSION = '0.001';

# A signal ends the program the ordinary way, so that the private temporary
# directories - a fresh key may be in one - are removed (SICK::Temp).
$SIG{HUP}  = sub { exit 129 };
$SIG{INT}  = sub { exit 130 };
$SIG{TERM} = sub { exit 143 };

exit SICK::CLI->new->run(@ARGV);

__END__

=head1 SYNOPSIS

    sick check                      # what does every device present?
    sick run                        # renew what is due
    sick run --dry-run              # only say what would be renewed
    sick run --force switch         # renew one device now, due or not
    sick trust /srv/www/trust       # root certificate and instructions for clients

    sick --config ./sick.yml check
    SICK_CONFIG=./sick.yml sick run --verbose

=head1 DESCRIPTION

C<sick> brings certificates of an internal certificate authority to the
devices of a list and keeps them current. The list is the whole
configuration (L<SICK::Config>); L<SICK> describes the idea.

C<sick> is no daemon. Renewal is C<sick run> in a timer or a cron job: a
run looks at every device and changes only those whose certificate is
missing, from another CA, for other names or about to expire.

    # /etc/cron.d/sick
    17 4 * * *  root  sick run; sick trust /srv/www/trust

=head1 COMMANDS

=head2 check [device ...]

Prints one line per device: which state its certificate is in and whether
a run would renew it. Changes nothing.

    DEVICE   STATUS       RENEW  DETAIL
    switch   ok           no     valid until 2027-11-06 03:02:18 UTC, 396 days left
    router   unreachable  ?      router.example.internal:443: Connection refused
    grafana  missing      yes    the device presents no certificate

The states are C<ok>, C<missing>, C<foreign-ca> (not from the CA of the
list), C<names> (not exactly the names of the list) and C<expiring> (less
than C<renew_before> left). C<unreachable> means that the device did not
answer - which is not the same as having no certificate - and C<error>
that something else kept SICK from looking, a device type without a module
for instance. Both leave the question of renewal open and make the exit
code C<1>.

=head2 run [--force] [--dry-run] [--verbose] [device ...]

For every device: check; if a new certificate is due, have it issued,
deploy it, and check that the device now presents exactly that
certificate.

    DEVICE   ACTION     REASON       ERROR
    switch   unchanged  ok
    router   failed     unreachable  unreachable: router.example.internal:443: Connection refused
    grafana  renewed    missing

The actions are C<unchanged>, C<renewed>, C<would-renew> (with
C<--dry-run>), C<failed> and C<deferred>. A device that fails does not stop
the others.

C<deferred> is a device whose last attempts failed after the decision to
renew - the CA refused or could not be reached, the deployment or the
second look failed. Its next attempt waits 1 hour after the first failure,
then 2, 4, 8, 16 and at most 24 hours, less a grace of 15 minutes so that
a daily timer does not skip a day; until then nothing is issued for it
and the error says
C<back-off after 3 failures, next attempt 2026-10-11 08:17:00 UTC>. It
counts as failed for the exit code. C<unchanged> or C<renewed> ends the
back-off; an unreachable device changes nothing in it. The certificate of a
failed attempt is not revoked: its key existed only in a private temporary
directory and is gone, so the certificate is useless and expires unused.

Only one C<sick run> works at a time: it takes the lock F<sick.lock> in the
state directory, and a second run ends with exit code C<3> and
C<another sick run holds the lock '/var/lib/sick/sick.lock' (pid 4242)>.
The lock goes with the process. C<check>, C<trust> and C<run --dry-run> take
no lock.

A run keeps one record per device in the state directory,
F<devices/E<lt>nameE<gt>.json>: the last look, the last certificate issued,
the last deployment, the failures in a row and the back-off - never a key
(L<SICK::Runner/"Records and back-off">).

=over 4

=item C<--force>, C<-f>

Renew also certificates that are fine, and devices in their back-off. A
device that cannot be reached still fails.

=item C<--dry-run>, C<-n>

Only look and say what would be renewed. Nothing is issued and nothing is
changed on any device.

=item C<--verbose>, C<-v>

Report on standard error what is being done, including what the targets
and the programs they call have to say.

=back

=head2 trust <directory>

Writes the root certificate of the CA into the directory, as PEM
(C<root_ca.pem>), as DER (C<root_ca.crt>) and as a configuration profile
for Apple devices (C<root_ca.mobileconfig>), install and remove scripts
(C<trust-linux.sh>, C<trust-macos.sh>, C<trust-windows.ps1>,
C<trust-nss.sh>), and an C<index.html> that shows the fingerprint and
explains installing and removing for Firefox, Chrome, Linux, macOS,
Windows, iOS and Android. Put the directory behind a web server and send
people the link - and the SHA-256 fingerprint of the root by another
channel: the page is not authenticated, and the scripts install nothing
unless they are given that fingerprint
(C<sudo sh trust-linux.sh install --fingerprint E<lt>sha256E<gt>>). See
L<SICK::Trust> and L<SICK::Trust::Scripts>.

When the C<ca> section names C<intermediates>, they are written as well:
C<intermediate_ca.pem>, C<intermediate_ca.crt> (DER) and C<ca_bundle.pem>,
the root and the intermediate in one file. A device that presents its
certificate without the intermediate - a switch with room for one
certificate - is refused by a client that knows the root alone. The profile
then carries the intermediate too, and the page says for each platform
where it goes.

During a replacement of the root, C<additional_roots> in the C<ca> section
names the further roots - the next one before the switch, the previous one
after it. They are written as C<root_ca_2.pem>, C<root_ca_2.crt> and so on,
join the root in C<ca_bundle.pem> and the profile, and the page names the
current root and lists the others.

The CA is called by the C<name> of the C<ca> section, or by the common name
of its root certificate.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 4

=item C<--config E<lt>fileE<gt>>, C<-c>

The device list. Without the option, the file named by the environment
variable C<SICK_CONFIG>; without that, F</etc/sick/sick.yml>.

=item C<--help>, C<-h>

Prints a summary of the commands and options.

=item C<--version>, C<-V>

Prints the version.

=back

=head1 EXIT CODES

=over 4

=item C<0>

Nothing failed.

=item C<1>

At least one device failed or was deferred (C<run>) or could not be checked
(C<check>), or C<trust> could not write its files, or C<run> could not use
its state directory.

=item C<2>

The command line or the device list is wrong: unknown command, option or
device, a list that cannot be read or is not valid.

=item C<3>

C<run> did nothing: another C<sick run> holds the lock of the state
directory.

=back

=head1 FILES

=over 4

=item F</var/lib/sick>

The state directory, C<state> in the device list: F<sick.lock> and
F<devices/E<lt>nameE<gt>.json> (L<SICK::State>).

=back

=head1 ENVIRONMENT

=over 4

=item C<SICK_CONFIG>

The device list, when C<--config> is not given.

=item C<SICK_OPENSSL>

The C<openssl> program to use (L<SICK::OpenSSL>).

=item C<TMPDIR>

Where the private temporary directories are made (L<SICK::Temp>).

=back

=cut
